      "latency_ms": 9071.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 1063,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance with the molecular formula **H₂O**, consisting of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. It is a fundamental component of life and exists in three primary states: **liquid** (common at room temperature), **solid** (ice, when frozen), and **gas** (water vapor, when heated). Here's a breakdown of its key characteristics and significance:\n\n### **Chemical and Physical Properties**\n- **Molecular Structure**: Water molecules are **polar** due to the uneven distribution of electrons, creating a slight positive charge on the hydrogen atoms and a negative charge on the oxygen. This polarity allows water to form hydrogen bonds, which influence its unique properties.\n- **States of Matter**: Water transitions between solid, liquid, and gas depending on temperature and pressure. For example, it freezes at 0°C (32°F) and boils at 100°C (212°F) at standard atmospheric pressure.\n- **High Specific Heat**: Water absorbs and releases heat slowly, helping regulate temperature in organisms and environments.\n- **Cohesion and Adhesion**: Water molecules stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion), enabling processes like capillary action in plants.\n\n### **Biological Importance**\n- **Essential for Life**: All known living organisms require water for survival. It acts as a **solvent** for biochemical reactions, transports nutrients and waste, and maintains cell structure.\n- **Body Function**: In humans, water makes up ~60% of the body, aiding in digestion, temperature regulation, and lubrication of joints.\n\n### **Environmental Role**\n- **Hydrological Cycle**: Water cycles through evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and runoff, sustaining ecosystems and weather patterns.\n- **Climate Regulation**: Oceans and water bodies moderate global temperatures and influence climate systems.\n\n### **Unique Characteristics**\n- **Density Anomaly**: Water is **denser as a liquid than as a solid** (ice floats), which is critical for aquatic life in cold climates.\n- **Universal Solvent**: Its polarity allows it to dissolve many substances, making it vital for chemical reactions in biology and industry.\n\nIn summary, water is a versatile and indispensable substance, shaping both the physical world and the processes of life. Its molecular properties and environmental role make it one of the most studied and essential compounds on Earth."
